==English==

===Etymology===
From {{m|en|take}} + {{m|en|aback}}, see the two entries for their respective etymology.

===Verb===
{{en-verb|takes aback|taking aback|took aback|taken aback}}

# {{lb|en|idiomatic|transitive}} To [[surprise]] or [[shock]]; to [[discomfit]].
#: ''I was rather '''taken aback''' by his angry reply.''
#: ''The bad news '''took''' us '''aback'''.''
#* {{quote-book|year=1807|title=Cobbett's Political Register, Vol. XI.|author=William Cobbett|location=London|publisher=Cox and Baylis|page=121|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=YLs7AQAAMAAJ&pg=PA121|passage=I was, at first, a little '''taken aback''' and astounded at the bulk of the volume; but, I turned out early this morning, and with eager hope and expectation set doggedly to work in search of the promised consolation.}}
#* {{quote-book|year=1808|title=The Post-Captain: A View of Naval Society and Manners|edition=3rd|location=London|publisher=Thomas Tegg|page=165|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=TOw1AQAAMAAJ&pg=PA165|passage=I would rather board a hundred of the enemy's frigates, than steer my boat into a fleet of modest women, for a modest woman never fails to '''take''' me '''aback'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|year=1998|title=The Beginning of Spring|author=Penelope Fitzgerald|location=Boston, New York|publisher=Houghton Mifflin Company|page=156|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=xjrxINUs87EC&pg=PA156|passage=Frank was '''taken aback''' when Lisa told him that she also needed forgiveness from him, for actions, for words, and for unspoken thoughts.}}
#* {{quote-book|year=2000|title=Soft!|author=Rupert Thomson|page=292|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=3aIo1jvQvekC&pg=PA292|passage=In a way, he was '''taken aback''' by the absence of discouragement.}}
#* {{quote-book|year=2000|title=White as Snow|author=Tanith Lee|location=New York|publisher=Tom Doherty Associates|page=105|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=KD_0onc8kQ0C&pg=PA105|passage=She was not '''taken aback''' to find he lived in a ramshackle log hut among the trees.}}
#* {{quote-book|year=2001|title=A Morning in Eden|author=Anna Gilbert|location=New York|publisher=St. Martin's Press|page=9|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=a1yLAwAAQBAJ&pg=PA9|passage=She was a little '''taken aback''' to find the front door of heavy oak unlocked.}}
#* {{quote-book|year=2002|title=Behind the Lines: The Oral History of Special Operations in World War II|author=Russell Miller|location=New York|publisher=St. Martin's Press|page=178|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=uIHAAwAAQBAJ&pg=PA178|passage=Zervas was rather '''taken aback''' to learn that Ares Veloutiotes, the communist leader, was on his way to the village because there was no friendship between them.}}
#* {{quote-book|year=2008|title=The Music of Life: Biology beyond genes|author=Denis Noble|page=123|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=Z9QaCmsSGf8C&pg=PA123|passage=The physiologist is '''taken aback'''.}}
#* '''2014''', Steve Rose, "[http://www.theguardian.com/film/2014/jul/01/dawn-of-the-planet-of-the-apes-review Dawn of the Planet of the Apes: a primate scream - first look review]", ''The Guardian'', 1 July 2014:
#*: They haven't seen humans for years, so when a small expedition, led by Jason Clarke, stumbles into apetopia, both sides are '''taken aback'''.
# {{lb|en|nautical|usually passive}} Of a ship: to catch it with the sails aback suddenly.

====Usage notes====
* Most commonly found in the [[passive]] voice.
* ''Aback'' is an adverb, so ''take aback'' is a separable phrasal verb.
* The use of the work ''take'' implies that the action is happening unexpectedly or against one's will. The sails of a ship are said to be ''taken aback'' by a change in the relative direction of the wind, but they are ''laid aback'' purposely by the crew. Likewise, someone may say he was ''taken aback'' when surprised.

